{
  "sections": {
    "founder": {
      "type": "custom-advertorial-kapoor",
      "blocks": {
        "hero": {
          "type": "hero_card",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Dr Richie Barclay · Founder",
            "heading": "Why I built a supplement you can measure",
            "standfirst": "<p>Fourteen years advising other people’s products taught me what the industry is willing to leave unproven. MUTO is what I wanted to exist instead.</p>",
            "credential": "PhD Molecular Biologist · Functional Medicine Nutritionist",
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/adv-founder-barclay.jpg",
            "image_alt": "Dr Richie Barclay",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "lede_open": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>I spent fourteen years as a consultant to supplement and health companies. I read the formulations, checked the claims against the research, and told brands what their product could honestly say. A lot of the time the answer was: less than you want to.</p><p>That work is where MUTO came from. Not from spotting a gap in the market - from being asked, repeatedly, to sign off on things I would not take myself.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "head_seeing": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "01 · What I kept seeing",
            "title": "The standard in this industry is lower than people assume.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_seeing": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Supplements are regulated as food, not medicine. That means a product can be legally sold without anyone ever demonstrating that it does what the packaging implies. The bar is safety and accurate labelling - not efficacy.</p><p>In practice that produces three habits I saw again and again: doses well below the ones used in the research being cited, cheap compound forms chosen for margin rather than absorption, and proprietary blends used to make both of those unverifiable. None of it is illegal. All of it means the customer is asked to take the claim on faith.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "seeing1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Underdosing",
            "title": "Citing a study, then including a fraction of the dose it used.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "seeing2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Cheap forms",
            "title": "Compounds picked for cost, not for how much of them you absorb.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "seeing3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "Proprietary blends",
            "title": "A legal way to list ingredients without saying how much of each.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_surface": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "02 · Surface and cellular",
            "title": "Most of the market sells the surface. The work happens underneath it.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_surface": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>My background is molecular biology, so I look at the layer beneath the outcome. Skin, energy, recovery, sleep quality - none of those are systems. They are results, produced by cells that need to make enough energy and repair enough damage to keep producing them.</p><p>A product aimed at the surface is aimed at the readout. It can improve how something looks or feels in the short term without touching what generates it. That is why so many stacks produce a strong first fortnight and then nothing: they were never working on the machinery, and the machinery is what declines with age.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "surface1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "Surface",
            "title": "The result you can see",
            "text": "<p>Skin, energy, recovery, how the morning feels. Worth caring about, but downstream of everything.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "surface2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "Cellular",
            "title": "The system producing it",
            "text": "<p>Mitochondria making energy and repair enzymes clearing damage - both funded by NAD⁺, which halves by your forties.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "statement_work": {
          "type": "statement",
          "settings": {
            "text": "The work you see starts with the work you don’t."
          }
        },
        "head_brief": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "03 · The design brief",
            "title": "Something you can track, not just trust.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_brief": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The thing that bothered me most about the category is that nobody can tell whether their supplements are working. You take them, you feel roughly the same, and you keep taking them because stopping feels like a risk. That is trust doing a job that evidence should be doing.</p><p>So the brief for MUTO was different: work on something measurable, in an area where people are already collecting data. Most of our customers wear a device that records HRV, resting heart rate and sleep every night. That is a personal dataset with months of baseline in it, and it is the honest way to judge a product like this.</p><p>Which is also why the guarantee runs to ninety days. It takes that long for a trend to mean anything, and I would rather you judged us on your own numbers than on how well I write.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "brief1": {
          "type": "brief_row",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Every dose printed",
            "text": "<p>At the level used in the research it comes from, so you can check the formula against the studies and against whatever else is in your cupboard.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "brief2": {
          "type": "brief_row",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Forms chosen for absorption",
            "text": "<p>Active folate and B12 rather than the cheap equivalents, plus BioPerine® so the rest of the formula is absorbed rather than passed through.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "brief3": {
          "type": "brief_row",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Reviewed by people who can say no",
            "text": "<p>A six-person scientific advisory board, all named with checkable credentials, who reviewed the formulation before manufacture.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "brief4": {
          "type": "brief_row",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Judged on your data",
            "text": "<p>Ninety days, and a refund if your numbers haven’t moved. The measurable version of a claim is the only version worth making.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
